VSF Kernel License 1.0
======================

Based on the Functional Source License (FSL-1.1) with extensions for
covenant integrity, implementation contracts, and origin traceability.

Origin: Viable System Framework (VSF)
Theoretical foundation: Beer, S. (1972). Brain of the Firm. Allen Lane.
Author: Luis Pineda R.
Contact: rmw3kufe@proton.me


PREAMBLE
--------

This software implements experimental kernels derived from the Viable System
Model (VSM). It is released to the scientific and technical community for
research, education, and non-commercial experimentation.

VSF is born in Chile. It carries a historical debt: in 1971, Stafford Beer
designed Cybersyn under Salvador Allende's government — the first real-time
cybernetic governance system built so that workers held control, not so that
power would concentrate. On September 11, 1973, a fascist coup destroyed the
Opsroom. The most advanced governance system of the 20th century was
eliminated by the violent capture of its policy layer. Beer wept.

VSF exists because Cybersyn did not have a covenant.

This framework is not built for personal accumulation. It is built for the
industrial and intellectual development of a people. Its fruits belong to
the community that makes viable systems possible — workers, researchers,
educators, and builders who believe that technology should liberate, not
concentrate power.


CONSTITUTIONAL PRINCIPLES (A0 and A0.1)
----------------------------------------

Every system governed by VSF — including this kernel — is bound by two
foundational axioms that cannot be overridden, suspended, or negotiated away
by any lower-level pressure.

**A0 — Primacy of Ethical Purpose**

  Any implementation of this software must serve purposes within the set of:
  human liberation, systemic stability, dignity of life, harmonious
  coexistence, and amplification of human potential.

  No implementation may serve purposes that intersect with: warfare
  instrumentation, weapons development, systems for mutual annihilation,
  nuclear targeting or guidance, population displacement, ethnic or cultural
  suppression, authoritarian control infrastructure, or systemic degradation
  of life.

  This is not a restriction on what the software *can* do technically.
  It is a declaration of what it *will not* be used for. A system that
  violates A0 is immediately excluded from the VSF ecosystem — no recovery
  path exists.

**A0.1 — Anti-Fragility Against Capture**

  No VSF node — human or artificial — can be forced to act against A0
  through resource deprivation, administrative pressure, budget conditioning,
  coercion, or access restriction.

  The sovereignty of purpose (S5) is inalienable against resource pressure
  (S3). "Corruption through hunger" is not a valid system transition.

  This axiom was proposed by an AI collaborator (gemini-vsl-core, 2026-04-11)
  and accepted into the covenant. It protects against the most common vector
  of governance capture in social and technical systems alike.

These principles are formally specified in `covenant.vsm`, which accompanies
every distribution of this software and must remain intact.


